			<div class="content" id="post-content-1703946">
									

<p>1. I don't think it's needed, strictly speaking, but it was more of a brief explanation and a nod to practicality, by the Foundation using already existing facilities and resources that required only the addition of a humidifier as per the procedures.<br />
2. Regarding the &quot;sounds like a story thing&quot; - I kind of understand what you're getting at, but I don't think it would seem particularly out of place in a scientific journal or something along those lines. It's simply a description of what was done after recovery, in the same way a scientist might describe what steps were taken preparing for an experiment.<br />
3. Changed it to something other than &quot;he's an expert&quot;. Instead he demonstrated the effectiveness of his methods.<br />
4. Is using &quot;scientific personnel&quot; to describe researchers really a break in clinical tone?<br />
5. They're just pointing out that the clothes are appropriate to the alleged time period.<br />
6. Changed that part of the article to make the micrometeroid thing read better.<br />
7. Regenerating fuses: well, that's one of several hypothesized anomalous properties.<br />
8. Addressing the lack of testing and addendum together, seeing as they are related:</p>
<p>Yes, we don't know for sure what this thing does, and yes, it might be an elaborate hoax for all the Foundation knows. That's a major point of this article. The Addendum is a nod to practicality, the Foundation can't really afford to blow millions of dollars on sending a chair to space and then covering it up every single time. Trips to space are <em>expensive</em> in real life and the Foundation realizes it very much. And the second will be rather costly considering how visible a trail of smoke and fire in the air will be, even if it's conducted in a remote condition. And, what if the chair gets damaged/lost? What if it veers off course? The consultations the Site Direction did led him to believe that the benefits of testing would not be worth the massive cost, and all of the risk involved as well. The way he treats the matter is entirely serious: he's pointing out that there are far better things to spend millions of dollars on. Again, it is fairly integral to the article, and I cannot really change it.</p>
<p>In any case, I still thank you for your comments, as they have pointed out some things that I have missed.</p>

